# Tests

The test layers are in `docs/design.ja.md` §12.1. This rule is how a test is written.

## Framework

- xUnit v3 with its built-in `Assert`. No assertion library.
- Tests run on Microsoft.Testing.Platform (`global.json` `test.runner`). The .NET 10 SDK refuses VSTest for these projects, so there is no `Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk` or `xunit.runner.visualstudio`. Run one project with `dotnet test --project <path>`, and some of its tests with `dotnet test --project <path> -- --filter-method <pattern>`. The test executable takes xUnit's own options (`-method`); given `--filter-method`, it prints `error: unknown option`, runs no test, and exits 3. In L1b a loop that looked only for failed tests counted 30 such runs as passes. Read the exit code and the count of tests run.
- Before calling a failing test a defect, look for what a mutation run leaves (`git status`, `*.orig` beside the source) and build again. In L1b a `wrap-reverse` test failed on a build that still had a mutant in it, and the defect it pointed to did not exist.
- A test of an invariant (`InvariantViolationException`) runs in the Debug configuration only, because release builds end the process. Start it with a `RequireDebugBuild()` helper that calls `Assert.Skip` when `DEBUG` is not defined (`tests/XylemUI.Core.Tests/ArenaTests.cs`). The gate runs both configurations.
- Process-wide state (`UiThread`, the native handle table, Win32 windows) is touched only from one dedicated thread per test assembly (`UiThreadFixture` in `tests/XylemUI.Platform.Windows.Tests`). xUnit v3 runs each test assembly in its own process.
- Time and frames come from a fake `TimeProvider` and the test frame scheduler (design §12.2). A test never sleeps, never waits on the wall clock, and never asserts what the clock decides.
- The exception is a test of how the OS itself waits and wakes (the Win32 message loop, S3). It is marked `[Trait("Category", "OsTiming")]`, waits in real time with bounds an order of magnitude above what was measured, and has a watchdog that wakes and fails a loop that does not finish, so that it cannot hang the run (`RunLoop` and `LoopWatchdog` in `tests/XylemUI.Platform.Windows.Tests/LoopTestHelpers.cs`).
- A test that wakes the loop from another thread owns that thread and joins it before the test ends (`WakeTicker` in `LoopTestHelpers.cs`). A wake queued to the thread pool outlives the test and reaches the next one: in S3 a leftover `ThreadPool` wake woke the idle test on CI, which ran the tests in another order than the local run, and the measured delay came out negative. A test that waits for its own wake also counts and asserts the iterations before it, so a stray wake fails loudly instead of being measured.
- The loop checks a test's done condition only when it wakes. When another thread sets that condition, it wakes the loop after setting it. Without the wake the loop stays blocked and the watchdog fails the test: the `Send` tests of S5 did this until the worker called `Win32MessageLoop.Wake` at its end.
- `Win32Window(title, width, height)` takes the outer size; the client area is smaller (200 x 100 gives 184 x 61). A test that reads pixels or places content by coordinates asserts `ClientSize` first (S7).
- `TestContext.Current` is async-local and does not flow to the fixture's UI thread. Read `TestContext.Current.TestOutputHelper` on the test thread before `ui.Invoke`. To see the output of a passing test, run the test executable with `-showLiveOutput`.
- Tests that need a GPU (layer 4) use WARP and run on Windows only; they are marked so the other layers run on every OS.
- Golden images and DisplayList snapshots render with the bundled fonts only (design §12.3).

## Structure

- `tests/<Project>.Tests` mirrors `src/<Project>`. `Foo.cs` → `FooTests.cs`, in the same folder.
- Method names: `{Target}_{Scenario}_{Expected}` in PascalCase, English.
- Each test owns its state. Tests run in parallel; a test that cannot (a real window, the UI thread) says why in its collection definition.

## Documentation

Every `[Fact]` and `[Theory]` has XML docs in this shape, in English:

```csharp
/// <summary>
/// One line: the behavior this test guards.
/// </summary>
/// <remarks>
/// Preconditions:
/// - ...
///
/// Steps:
/// - ...
///
/// Expected:
/// - ...
/// </remarks>
[Fact]
public void Dispose_NestedScopes_RunsChildrenThenOwnCleanupsInLifoOrder()
{
}
```

- A `[Theory]` is documented once; list what the cases cover under Preconditions or Steps.
- No custom XML tags. Link a design section when the why is not obvious.

## What a test checks

- A test stands for the whole design statement (`.cursor/rules/spec-checks.mdc`).
- The expected value of a subtle CSS result (fit-content, an automatic minimum, a percentage of an indefinite size) comes from an outside reference, not from reasoning: Chrome's result through a Taffy fixture, or the same tree laid out from the start. In L4 a test expected a fit-content width of 30 that the engine, matching the fixtures, gives as 50, and the test had to go (PR #103).
- Compare with an independent reference: a hand-computed layout, the Unicode test files, a golden image produced by an earlier reviewed run. Do not compare a function with a re-implementation of itself.
- A random test of incremental work puts several changes, and changes that keep a size but not what a parent reads, before one pass. In L5 the test first made one change per pass, and its "same size" change changed nothing; once it changed 1 to 3 leaves, a leaf that an ancestor laid out first gave a wrong rectangle, which no single change can reach (PR #112, #115).
- A tolerance (pixel difference, float comparison) is a fixed number written in the test, with the reason in a comment when it is not the default.
- The setup that a mutant needs reaches the branch it changes, and the test asserts that it does. In S8a an atlas "full" for 28-pixel glyphs still had room for a 0-pixel blank glyph, so the mutant that made blanks take room passed; the test now checks where the next glyph lands. In S9 a scope's cleanup ran with the current scope of the code that disposed it, so a test that disposed from outside never reached the "a scope being disposed takes no children" branch; the test now disposes the scope from its own body.
- A surviving mutant is first a question about the setup (the item above). When the branch cannot be reached at all, show why and remove the branch instead of listing the mutant (`.cursor/rules/no-dead-code.mdc`): in S9 relinking a computed value always follows a refresh at the same global version, so the code that marked it dirty there went.
- A test of a comparison (a least-recently-used pick, a priority, a tie-break) sets up candidates whose values differ. In S8b every plot of the eviction test had the same age, so the plot picked first was also the oldest, and the mutant that picked the newest passed; the test now gives the plots three different ages.
- A pixel test compares the frame with an image composed on the CPU from the same inputs in the display list's order, not each element with the background around it. Neighbouring glyph boxes overlap, so in S8b a check of the ring around each glyph failed on the next glyph's ink.
- Mutation check for a guard: change the guarded term and see the test fail, then restore it by copying the file aside and back (not `git checkout`). List the mutations in the PR.
- A mutant must build. With warnings as errors, a mutant such as `if (false)` fails to compile (CS0162), `dotnet test --no-build` then runs the previous binaries, and the mutant looks survived. Check the build's exit code before reading the test result, and write the mutant so that it compiles (`if (IsActive && count < 0)`) (S4). A style analyzer fails the build too: in S5 a mutant with a trailing space (SA1028) did not build. A mutant run that names no failing test counts as a failed build until its build output says otherwise. Read this item before writing the mutants: in S8b the first mutants were `if (true)` again and did not build.
- A script that runs mutants decides from the exit code of `dotnet test`, not from its text: Microsoft.Testing.Platform exits with 2 when a test failed, and its messages follow the OS language. `python -I` ignores `PYTHONIOENCODING`; a script that prints test output calls `sys.stdout.reconfigure(encoding="utf-8", errors="replace")` (S8b).
- A mutant that would dereference null in unsafe code is not run: it ends the test process and the other tests do not run. List it in the PR with that reason. A mutant that only leaks a COM reference cannot be caught without the D3D11 debug layer; say so too (S7).
- Every test of a contract the design states gets that mutation check before the PR, not only the guards a review names. A test that passes against the mutant passes for another reason, and the reason is usually the test's own setup or cleanup.
- Read the state you assert before a shared helper's cleanup runs. A helper that resets state on the way out (`RunLoop` cancels the pending frame in its `finally`) makes an assertion after it check the helper, not the code. Record the state inside the run, where the code under test left it.

```csharp
// BAD — RunLoop's finally calls CancelFrame, which stops the clock: this passes even when RunFrame never stops it (S3)
RunLoop(watchdog, () => runs >= 1, () => frames.RequestFrame(callback));
Assert.False(frames.IsClockRunning);

// GOOD — read the clock in the iteration after the frame, before the cleanup
bool? runningAfterFrame = null;
RunLoop(watchdog, () => { if (runs < 1) return false; runningAfterFrame = frames.IsClockRunning; return true; }, start);
Assert.False(runningAfterFrame);
```
